Here are practical ways to prove the Function App’s Dataverse Managed Identity (MI) is working, using what you already have in this project.

What “MI is working” means
Three things must be true:

Function App MI acquires a token to Dataverse (DefaultAzureCredential → audience = your org URL).
Application User in Power Platform is linked to that MI and has a security role.
CallerId impersonation runs Dataverse queries as the signed-in user (not as a shared secret).
If MI were broken, you would see failures at startup (ServiceClient.IsReady = false) or on the first Dataverse call — not successful policy/opportunity reads.

Evidence package for InfoSec
1. Configuration proof (static)
Check	Where	What to show
MI enabled
Azure Portal → Function App → Identity
System-assigned = On; note Object (principal) ID
No CRM secret
Function App → Configuration
No CRM_CLIENT_ID / CRM_CLIENT_SECRET; only CRM_URL
App user exists
Power Platform admin → Application users
User linked to the MI object ID with a security role
Code uses MI
DataverseServiceClientFactory.cs
DefaultAzureCredential() + Dataverse scope — no client secret
2. Successful API call (functional proof)
Run a real request (Postman collection or D365 validator):

Policy: getPolicyData with valid auth + a user who can read the policy
EApp: getSOReqForEappSSO with valid token + userId/oid
If MI + App User work: HTTP 200 and business data returned.

If MI is broken: you typically get 500 at connection time (Failed to connect to CRM) or 403 from Dataverse before upstream APIs run.

Policy only reaches upstream APIs after DataverseAccessGuard succeeds — so a 200 with policy JSON implies MI + impersonation worked.

3. Application Insights logs (best audit trail)
Your functions already log impersonation, e.g.:

EApp: "CRM connection ready.", "Impersonating Dataverse user: {UserId}"
Policy: "Impersonating Dataverse user {UserId} ({UserName}) to verify policy access."
KQL — prove Dataverse was hit on a request:

traces
| where timestamp > ago(7d)
| where message contains "Impersonating Dataverse user"
    or message contains "CRM connection ready"
| project timestamp, message, operation_Id, customDimensions
| order by timestamp desc
KQL — tie one HTTP request to user + outcome (from docs/AppInsights-KQL-Queries.txt):

requests
| where timestamp > ago(7d)
| where name contains "getPolicyData" or name contains "getSOReqForEappSSO"
| extend oid = tostring(customDimensions["oid"])
| project timestamp, name, resultCode, success, duration, oid, operation_Id
| order by timestamp desc
Export query #6 from docs/AppInsights-KQL-Queries.txt for an InfoSec-friendly CSV.

4. Azure AD sign-in logs (MI token acquisition)
In Entra ID → Sign-in logs, filter:

Application: Function App MI (service principal name)
Resource: Dataverse / Dynamics (https://<org>.crm.dynamics.com or similar)
Status: Success
That shows the platform identity obtained a Dataverse token without a stored secret.

5. Negative test (strongest proof for security reviewers)
Controlled test in non-prod:

Step	Action	Expected
A
Disable/remove Application User role (or disable app user)
Next API call fails (connection or privilege error in App Insights)
B
Re-enable role
Same call succeeds again
This demonstrates access is tied to MI + App User, not a static credential.

6. Impersonation proof (CallerId)
In logs/traces for a 403 test:

Use a user without access to the policy/opportunity.
You should see impersonation logged, then 403 / “Access denied” — meaning MI connected, but Dataverse enforced user-level security.
That separates:

MI failure → can’t connect at all
Impersonation working → connect OK, user denied on record
7. Unit tests (design assurance)
You already have tests that assert CallerId is set and reset:

KOFC-FA-AgentPortal-UserProfile.Tests/EappSSOTests.cs
KOFC-FA-PolicyNonHeader-TierOne.Tests/DataverseAccessGuardTests.cs
These show the pattern is implemented correctly (InfoSec often accepts this as dev evidence, not runtime proof).

One-page story for InfoSec
The Function App has no Dataverse client secret. It uses system-assigned Managed Identity to authenticate to Dataverse. The MI is registered as an Application User with least-privilege roles. On each request, the function sets CallerId to the signed-in user’s systemuserid so Dataverse enforces that user’s record access. Evidence: (1) MI enabled + no CRM secrets in config, (2) Entra sign-in logs for MI → Dataverse, (3) App Insights traces showing impersonation + successful/failed access, (4) negative test when App User role is removed.

